[17592186555725 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555725 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555725 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555725 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555725 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555725 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ad4e7b48-b967-5cd8-8d67-9aafb4784107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555741 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555741 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555741 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555741 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555741 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d3ce3bff-654b-53ac-b60d-98e14aab9c6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555750 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555750 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555750 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555750 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555750 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555750 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555750 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555750 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555750 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e8da3860-d059-5229-bff4-f91bfdfe7bd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555770 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555770 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161603" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555770 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555770 #edb/ref 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=\\sqrt{25-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555770 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "20069222-ef1d-5746-8188-77095ae40eed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555770 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/5d/5d8a94cd0df8095c78aec955c3d0f062e38410326399104644b24e981eb1db9a.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{25-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}zr\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555773 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555773 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161628" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555773 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555773 #edb/ref 1071 "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,y-4\\le z\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555773 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22f18443-8c16-5b1a-8aa4-cdb902d79223" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555773 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/a6/a6b6faca2cd9cb66d463178a24efeb6f47d1dfa0bffe17c657e0d0be2dd11426.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y-4=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}zr\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555811 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555811 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161631" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555811 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555811 #edb/ref 1071 "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,-y-3\\le z\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}V=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555811 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d6469f2-3310-5668-93b9-289e45866c22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555811 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b1/b1886315cede967bc6fd606cdf7bf9d6ae4f82264ccce22211562aa590a8fe4a.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-y-3=-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}({(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+1)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}({r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555819 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555819 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161609" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555819 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555819 #edb/ref 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ between the surfaces $z=1$ and $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555819 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d03cab7-f35e-5f26-85d3-e871e46cdf45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555819 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/14/140ac7f7d6036657a4ba8a0c6944474c2aa0dfba7f7719228cdffb4d0cf9316f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-quarter of a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4={r}^{2}+4.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1\\le z\\le {r}^{2}+4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{{r}^{2}+4}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555841 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555841 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161602" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555841 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555841 #edb/ref 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ between the surfaces $z=2$ and $z=4+y.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555841 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ff4f4d5-934d-5904-8c7d-edf6b1d6934c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555841 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/28/286910d89e35603dc6558b80b11d06736d213fbad8c9c91825f42ff87b26e318.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=2,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=4+y=4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{5},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,2\\le z\\le 4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{z}^{2}r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{2}^{4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
